var e={id:`sysdesign-l2-case-video-uber`,subjectId:`sysdesign`,level:2,title:`Case Studies: YouTube/Netflix & Uber`,whyItMatters:`Two of the four most-asked design prompts, and they pull in opposite directions: a video platform is the most read-heavy system you will ever draw, while ride-hailing is a write firehose that must answer "who is near me?" in milliseconds. Doing both back to back proves the point interviewers are testing — the method is fixed, the answer is not. In each case one estimation number, computed in the first five minutes, forces the architecture.`,estMinutes:65,sections:[{type:`intuition`,title:`Two systems, one method`,md:`You get 45 minutes and a one-line prompt: "design YouTube". Candidates fail by immediately naming components. Candidates pass by following a method.

- The method, every time: **requirements → estimation → API → data model → high-level design → deep-dive two components → bottlenecks and tradeoffs**.
- Estimation is not arithmetic for its own sake. **One number always shapes the design.** Find it early and the rest falls out.
- For a video platform that number is **egress bandwidth**. For ride-hailing it is the **location write rate**. Neither is the number a beginner reaches for.
- Both walkthroughs below are written so you can attempt each step first. When you hit a question in bold, stop and answer it before reading on.
- Nothing here is memorised architecture. Every box exists because a number forced it — and when the interviewer changes the numbers, the boxes change too.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Part A — YouTube/Netflix: what are we actually building?`,md:`**Attempt it: list the functional and non-functional requirements before reading on.**

- Functional: **upload** a video, **transcode** it, **stream** it to any device, **search** by title and metadata, **recommend** what to watch next.
- Non-functional, and these are the ones that shape the design: **startup latency** under about 2 seconds (a spinner loses the viewer), **no buffering** mid-playback, a **global** audience, and devices from a 240p phone on 2G to a 4K TV.
- The traffic shape: **massive read skew** — roughly one upload per 10,000 views. Optimise the read path ruthlessly; the write path is allowed to be slow and asynchronous.
- Explicitly out of scope in 45 minutes: the recommendation model itself ("a separate ML pipeline writes a ranked candidate list per user; I will treat it as a service"), DRM, and monetisation.
- Cutting scope out loud is a senior signal. Trying to design all of it is how candidates run out of time at minute 40 with no CDN drawn.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Estimation: which number shapes this design?`,md:`**Attempt it: 500,000 uploads a day, roughly 1 GB of source video each. How much storage per year — and is storage what this system actually costs?**

- Transcoding is a **multiplier**. You keep the source plus a ladder of renditions (240p, 360p, 480p, 720p, 1080p, 4K) with their audio tracks. Call it 3× the source, conservatively.
- 500,000 × 1 GB × 3 = **1.5 PB of new storage per day**, about 0.5 EB per year. A big number — and a boring one, because storage is cheap and paid once.
- Now the read side: 500M daily users × 40 minutes each × ~3 Mbps average bitrate. That is the arithmetic below, and it lands two orders of magnitude above the storage figure.
- The rule this teaches: in any content-delivery system, **estimate what you send before you estimate what you keep**. Sending is paid on every view; keeping is paid once.`},{type:`code`,lang:`python`,title:`Egress vs storage — do the arithmetic, not the adjectives`,code:`GB = 1024 ** 3

# STORE: 500k uploads/day, ~1 GB of source each, x3 for the ladder + the source
stored = 500_000 * 1.0 * 3.0                 # GB/day

# SEND: 500M daily users x 40 min/day at ~3 Mbps average bitrate
egress = 500e6 * (40 * 60) * 3e6 / 8 / GB    # GB/day

print("stored/day  : %7.0f TB" % (stored / 1024))
print("egress/day  : %7.0f PB   = %.0fx the bytes we added" % (egress / 1024 / 1024, egress / stored))
print("peak pipe   : %7.0f Tbps (2x the daily average)" % (egress * GB * 8 / 86400 / 1e12 * 2))
print("egress  /yr : $%5.2f B  @ $0.01/GB CDN" % (egress * 0.01 * 365 / 1e9))
print("storage /yr : $%5.2f B  @ $0.02/GB-month" % (stored * 365 * 0.02 * 12 * 0.5 / 1e9))

# --- real output ---
# stored/day  :    1465 TB
# egress/day  :     400 PB   = 279x the bytes we added
# peak pipe   :      83 Tbps (2x the daily average)
# egress  /yr : $ 1.53 B  @ $0.01/GB CDN
# storage /yr : $ 0.07 B  @ $0.02/GB-month`,annotations:{4:`The transcoding multiplier IS the storage story: one upload becomes a ladder of renditions, and you keep the source so you can re-encode into a new codec later.`,7:`Mbps to bytes: divide by 8. This single line decides the architecture — every box downstream exists to serve it.`,11:`83 Tbps at peak. No origin cluster serves that. The CDN is not an optimisation in this design, it is the design.`,17:`We send 279x more bytes per day than we add, at roughly 23x the annual cost — and unlike a one-off storage purchase, egress is re-paid at full price every year.`}},{type:`note`,md:`**The sentence to say out loud: "storage is a rounding error; bandwidth is the business."** Three consequences follow immediately, and stating them buys you the rest of the design. (1) Every architectural choice is judged by whether it removes bytes from the origin — so **CDN cache-hit ratio is the cost model**, and a 96% → 98% hit ratio is a 50% cut in origin traffic. (2) Compute for transcoding is real but paid **once per video**, while delivery is paid **once per view** — that ratio is why nobody optimises the encoder first. (3) Because storage is cheap, you can afford to keep the original source forever; because bandwidth is not, you cannot afford to serve one unpopular rendition badly.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`API and data model — the small part, done fast`,md:"**Attempt it: name four endpoints, and say what the videos table holds. Then say what it does NOT hold.**\n\n- `POST /videos` → returns a video_id and an **upload URL**. That URL is **pre-signed**: a time-limited, single-purpose link letting the client PUT bytes straight into blob storage, so a gigabyte never traverses your application servers (mechanics are in the L0 blob-storage and CDN module).\n- `GET /videos/{id}` → metadata plus the **manifest URL**. Note it returns a manifest, not a video file — the reason is the next section.\n- `GET /search?q=...` → served by a search index (Elasticsearch-style), never by a LIKE query on the primary database.\n- `POST /videos/{id}/view` → fire-and-forget onto a queue. Never a synchronous `UPDATE videos SET views = views + 1`.\n- Data model: `videos(video_id, uploader_id, title, description, duration, status, created_at)` in a relational store — a few hundred million small rows, needing transactions at publish time and queried a hundred different ways. Beside it: one row per rendition and the object-storage key prefix for its segments.\n- The line that matters: **the video bytes are never in the database.** The database stores keys into blob storage. Confusing those two is the classic junior answer."},{type:`intuition`,title:`The upload pipeline — the centrepiece of this design`,md:`**Attempt it: a user uploads a 1 GB file. Trace it to the moment a viewer on another continent can press play.**

1. Client calls \`POST /videos\`. The API writes a row with \`status = uploading\` and returns a **pre-signed URL**. Bytes flow client → blob storage directly, resumable in chunks so a dropped connection does not restart a gigabyte.
2. Blob storage fires an **object-created event**. That event enqueues a transcoding job on a durable queue. Upload and processing are now **decoupled**: the transcoding fleet can be entirely down and uploads still succeed.
3. **Transcoding workers** pull jobs, split the source, and encode the **rendition ladder** — several resolutions × bitrates, plus audio and thumbnails.
4. Each rendition is **segmented**: cut into 2–10 second chunks, all renditions aligned on the same timestamps, and a **manifest** listing them is written.
5. Segments and manifests are written to blob storage; the CDN pulls from it on first request (or is pre-warmed for a known-hot release). Metadata flips to \`status = ready\` and the video goes live.

The pipeline is asynchronous by construction. The user's upload returns in seconds; the video appears minutes later. That "processing…" delay is not a flaw — it is the price you deliberately paid to keep a multi-minute CPU job off the request path.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Why transcoding fans out into parallel chunk jobs`,md:`Encoding a one-hour 4K source into six renditions on a single worker takes hours. Splitting it into chunks and running 200 workers takes minutes. Three separate reasons, and interviewers want all three.

- **Latency**: a two-hour job is a two-hour publish delay. Chunked, the same work finishes in the time of the slowest chunk plus a stitch.
- **Failure blast radius**: a long job that dies at 95% restarts from zero. A chunk job that dies costs 10 seconds of work and one retry.
- **Elasticity**: chunks are stateless units on a queue, so worker count tracks the backlog — and spot/preemptible instances become usable. They are cheap precisely because they can vanish, which only stateless short jobs tolerate.
- The catch to volunteer: chunk boundaries must land on **keyframes** (a GOP — group of pictures — decodes independently), or the stitched output stutters at every seam.
- The other catch: a final **stitch and package** step waits for every chunk, so one straggler holds the whole video. Mitigate by speculatively re-running the slowest outstanding chunk on a second worker and taking whichever finishes first.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Adaptive bitrate: why quality changes mid-video`,md:`A restaurant with one portion size either starves the hungry or wastes food. Adaptive bitrate serves a new portion every few seconds, sized to how fast you are actually eating.

- **HLS** (Apple) and **DASH** (open standard) do the same thing: the video exists as short segments at every rung of the bitrate ladder, all cut at identical timestamps so a player can switch rungs between segments.
- The **manifest** (\`.m3u8\` or \`.mpd\`) is a plain text file listing every rung and every segment URL. It is the only clever part of the format, it is downloaded first, and it is why your API hands out a manifest URL rather than a video URL.
- **The client decides.** It times how long the last segment took to arrive, estimates its bandwidth, and requests the next segment at the highest rung it can sustain. The server has no opinion and no session state.
- That is why quality drops when you walk into a lift and recovers afterwards — and why playback does not stop: the player degrades quality instead of buffering.
- Why this design scales: segments are **immutable, cacheable, ordinary HTTP files**. No streaming protocol, no per-viewer state on the server, so any dumb CDN edge can carry the entire load.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`The CDN is the whole delivery story`,md:`Your origin must never serve a video byte to a viewer. At 83 Tbps peak this is not a preference, it is arithmetic.

- A **CDN** is a fleet of caches in hundreds of cities. The first request for a segment in Mumbai pulls it from origin; the next hundred thousand are served from a box a few milliseconds away — which is also how you hit the 2-second startup target globally.
- **Cache-hit economics**: at a 95% hit ratio, origin still serves 5% of 400 PB/day = 20 PB/day. Move to 98% and origin traffic drops by 60% for the same catalogue. Hit ratio is the single number to optimise.
- It works because popularity is brutally skewed: a tiny fraction of videos is most of the traffic. Cache-hit ratio is therefore a property of your **catalogue shape**, not your hardware.
- **Netflix Open Connect** is the extreme version: Netflix builds its own appliances, gives them to ISPs to install inside their networks, and pre-loads them overnight with what that region will watch tomorrow. Prediction replaces caching; the bytes never cross a paid transit link.
- The tradeoff worth naming: that is a hardware business, staff, and logistics — justified only because egress is the largest line item on the bill. At a smaller scale, paying a commercial CDN per GB is obviously right.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Metadata, search, and the view-counter trap`,md:`The database side of a video platform is a small problem wearing a big system's clothes — except for one row that ruins everything.

- Metadata lives in a relational store, sharded by video_id only if it ever needs to be. Millions of small rows, not petabytes. Search is a separate inverted index; recommendations are a precomputed list per user, refreshed offline.
- The naive counter: \`UPDATE videos SET views = views + 1 WHERE id = ?\` on every play. On a viral video that is thousands of writes per second **to a single row** — and every one of them queues behind the same row lock.
- This is the classic **hot row** failure. It does not just slow the counter; the lock queue and the transaction backlog degrade unrelated queries on the same database.
- The fix, in one line: **the view event goes onto a queue, and the counter is written by an aggregator, in batches.** Deep dive below.
- The price: a view count that is a few seconds stale, and that can briefly move backwards after a failover. Say it plainly — **a view count is a metric, not a bank balance.**`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Deep dive 1 — what happens when transcoding fails`,md:`**Attempt it: a chunk job crashes at 60%. What must already be true for the system to recover with no human involved?**

- Jobs live on a durable queue with **at-least-once delivery**. A worker that dies without acknowledging simply has its job redelivered — the system depends on the queue, never on a worker staying alive.
- At-least-once means duplicates, so every chunk job must be **idempotent**: it writes to a deterministic path (\`video_id/rendition/chunk_0042.ts\`), so a re-run overwrites identical bytes. No appends, no counter increments, no "insert a new row per attempt".
- Retries use **exponential backoff with jitter**. A failure caused by an overloaded cluster gets strictly worse if ten thousand jobs retry in the same second.
- A genuinely corrupt source file would retry forever, so cap attempts (about 3) and route the job to a **dead letter queue**: the video is marked \`failed\`, the uploader is told, and an engineer sees a real bug instead of an infinite loop.
- State tracking: one row per chunk (pending / running / done). The stitch step fires only when all chunks are done, and any chunk stuck in \`running\` past a timeout is re-queued — which is safe precisely because the job is idempotent.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Deep dive 2 — view counts at hundreds of thousands of events per second`,md:`The pipeline that replaces the hot row, and the three things that go wrong inside it.

- **Ingest**: the player emits a view event to a queue (Kafka), partitioned by video_id so all events for one video land in order on one partition. Writing to a log is a sequential append — the cheapest write a computer does.
- **Aggregate**: a stream job keeps in-memory counters per video over a window (say 10 seconds) and emits one \`+N\` per video per window. Thousands of writes per second become one. The database update is now \`views = views + N\`, run once per window.
- **Hot-key skew**: one video going viral means one partition carrying the whole load. Fix by **salting** the key (video_id plus a random 0–15 suffix) into 16 partitions and summing the 16 sub-counters on read — the standard hot-key remedy.
- **Deduplication and fraud**: at-least-once delivery plus bots means raw counts are inflated. Deduplicate on a (video_id, viewer, session) key inside the window, and count a "view" only after a watch-time threshold. Unique-viewer counts use a **HyperLogLog** sketch — approximate, but kilobytes instead of a set of a billion ids.
- **Two pipelines, honestly**: fast-and-approximate feeds the UI counter; a slow, exact, replayable batch job over the same event log produces the number that pays a creator. The event log is the source of truth, and both pipelines are just views over it.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Bottlenecks and tradeoffs (Part A)`,md:`- **Cold content**: the long tail is a cache miss by definition — every play of a five-year-old video hits origin. Do not try to cache it; accept a slower start for the tail and keep the cache for content that repeats.
- **Thundering herd on a premiere**: a scheduled release means a million players request segment 1 in the same second and all miss simultaneously. Two fixes: **pre-warm** the CDN before release, and **request coalescing** at the edge — the first miss fetches from origin and everyone else waits on that single fetch instead of stampeding it.
- **Storage lifecycle**: most videos are never watched again. Six renditions each is waste. Tier unpopular videos to cold storage, delete their rarely-requested high renditions, and regenerate on demand from the source you kept — which is exactly why you kept it.
- **The re-encode problem**: a newer codec can halve the bandwidth bill, but re-encoding the whole catalogue costs a fortune in compute. Popularity-weighted is the answer: re-encode the top 1% of videos and capture most of the saving.
- **What you traded**: publish latency of minutes, and counters that are eventually consistent, in exchange for a read path that survives 400 PB a day. Both are cheap for this product — but on **live** streaming, publish latency is the product, and that inverts the design (short segments, low-latency HLS, and a much less forgiving pipeline).`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Part B — Uber: requirements`,md:`**Attempt it: what must the system do, and which non-functional requirement is the hard one?**

- Functional: a rider requests a ride from a pickup point; the system **matches** a nearby driver; both sides see **live location**; **ETA** and **price** are shown before confirming.
- Non-functional: **match latency** in seconds (a spinner past ~10 seconds loses the ride), an enormous **write rate** of location updates, and **geographic locality** — a ride in Bengaluru never involves data in Berlin, so regional sharding is natural and nearly free.
- Consistency: availability wins nearly everywhere. A driver marker two seconds stale is fine; showing no drivers is not. There is exactly **one** place that needs strict correctness — two riders must never be assigned the same driver.
- Out of scope in 45 minutes: payments, fraud, driver onboarding, ratings. Name them and move on.
- The shape: **write-heavy, low-latency, spatial** — the mirror image of Part A in every dimension. Same seven steps, completely different answer.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Estimation: the number that shapes Uber`,md:`**Attempt it: 1 million drivers online, each pinging their location every 4 seconds. What is the write QPS — and how does it compare to the rate of actual rides?**

- Location writes: 1,000,000 ÷ 4 = **250,000 writes per second**, sustained, whether the city is busy or asleep.
- Rides: 1 million rides/day ≈ 12 per second average, perhaps 50/s at peak.
- The ratio is about **5,000 : 1**. The business event is rare; the telemetry is a firehose. Design for the firehose.
- Payload sanity check: ~50 bytes per ping × 250k/s ≈ 12.5 MB/s. Bandwidth is trivial. What hurts is **index churn** — the entire spatial index is effectively rewritten every four seconds.
- So the design question is not "how do we handle rides", it is "how do we absorb a quarter-million tiny writes per second and still answer *who is near this point* in a few milliseconds".`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Why the obvious query fails`,md:'**Attempt it: what exactly is wrong with `SELECT * FROM drivers WHERE lat BETWEEN ? AND ? AND lon BETWEEN ? AND ?`**\n\n- A B-tree index is **one-dimensional**. An index on `lat` finds every driver in a latitude band — and that band wraps the entire planet. The database then filters by longitude row by row.\n- A composite `(lat, lon)` index does not save you: the second column only narrows within one exact value of the first, and latitude is a continuous float. Effectively every lookup is that global strip.\n- Now combine that with a table being rewritten 250,000 times a second: the index maintenance cost and the scan cost fight each other, and both lose.\n- The insight behind every geospatial index: **fold two dimensions into one ordered key**, so "near me" becomes a range scan. Geohashing, quadtrees, S2 and H3 are four ways to do that one thing.'},{type:`intuition`,title:`Geohashing: two dimensions folded into one string`,md:`Think of the world as a chessboard. Cut it in half, note which half. Cut that half, note which half. Keep going and the sequence of answers names a box.

- **Geohash** interleaves the bits: one bit of longitude, one of latitude, one of longitude, and so on. Each bit halves a range. Every 5 bits become one base32 character.
- The consequence: **nearby points share a prefix**, and more shared characters means a smaller shared box — 6 characters is roughly 1.2 km, 7 characters roughly 150 m.
- So "drivers near me" becomes "keys starting with \`tdr1vf\`" — a **prefix range scan on a single ordered key**, which every ordered store (and a Redis sorted set) does well.
- Precision is a tuning knob: choose the character length so a cell holds a useful number of drivers — a shorter prefix in the countryside, a longer one downtown.
- The honest edge case, and it is the one interviewers push on: two points ten metres apart on opposite sides of a cell boundary share almost no prefix. **Prefix length is not distance.** The demo below proves it with real coordinates.`},{type:`code`,lang:`python`,title:`Geohash in 18 lines — and its boundary problem, live`,code:`B32 = '0123456789bcdefghjkmnpqrstuvwxyz'

def geohash(lat, lon, precision=7):
    lat_r, lon_r = [-90.0, 90.0], [-180.0, 180.0]
    bits = ch = 0
    out = []
    even = True                              # lon bit, lat bit, lon bit, ...
    while len(out) < precision:
        rng, val = (lon_r, lon) if even else (lat_r, lat)
        mid = (rng[0] + rng[1]) / 2
        if val > mid:
            ch, rng[0] = (ch << 1) | 1, mid  # upper half -> bit 1
        else:
            ch, rng[1] = ch << 1, mid        # lower half -> bit 0
        even, bits = not even, bits + 1
        if bits == 5:                        # 5 bits -> one base32 char
            out.append(B32[ch]); bits = ch = 0
    return ''.join(out)

rider = (12.9752, 77.6068)                   # MG Road, Bengaluru
print('rider ', geohash(*rider))
print('D1    ', geohash(12.9762, 77.6073), '  124 m away')
print('D2    ', geohash(12.9789, 77.6081), '  435 m away')
print('D3    ', geohash(12.9985, 77.5990), ' 2729 m away')

# --- real output ---
# rider  tdr1vfp
# D1     tdr1vfp   124 m away      -> shares all 7 chars: same cell
# D2     tdr1y48   435 m away      -> shares only 4: it fell over a boundary
# D3     tdr1vy3  2729 m away      -> shares 5, though it is 6x further than D2`,annotations:{9:`The interleave: alternate the axis every bit. This is the whole trick — it is what makes one string encode two dimensions.`,12:`Each bit answers "upper or lower half of the current range?". Seven characters is 35 bits, which pins a point to roughly a 150 m box.`,23:`D2 is the lesson. It is nearer than D3, yet shares fewer prefix characters, because a cell boundary runs between it and the rider.`,30:`Read this twice: prefix similarity is NOT distance. A query on the rider cell alone would miss a driver 435 m away while happily returning one 2.7 km away.`}},{type:`note`,md:`**The fix for boundaries: query 9 cells, not 1.** Compute the rider's cell plus its 8 surrounding neighbours, union the results, then filter by true distance. Every production geo lookup does this, and a candidate who does not mention it always gets the follow-up. The alternatives, one line each. **Quadtree**: recursively split a square into four until each leaf holds at most N points, so cells adapt to density — fine cells downtown, one huge cell over the sea — at the cost of a tree you must maintain as points move. **S2** (Google): projects the sphere onto a cube and orders cells along a Hilbert space-filling curve, giving near-equal cell areas and exact neighbour arithmetic. **H3** (Uber's own): hexagons, where every neighbour centre is the same distance away — unlike a square grid, whose diagonal neighbours are 1.4× further — which makes radius queries and surge zones behave sanely. H3 exists because Uber ran into geohash's edge cases at scale.`},{type:`note`,md:`**Why the diagram below is the matching flow and not the video pipeline.** The upload pipeline is a straight line — upload, event, fan out, stitch, publish — and a straight line reads better as the numbered list you already have than as frames. The matching flow is not a line: it has a *wrong* version (query one cell, miss the driver 435 m away) and a *race* (two riders, one driver) that only become obvious when you can see the same state at two different moments. That is exactly what a stepper is for, so the pipeline stays in prose and the frames go to matching.`},{type:`visual`,component:`PointerBoxDiagram`,props:{title:`Geo-matching: cells fill, a request queries, one driver is claimed`,notice:`Left: incoming pings and ride requests. Right: the in-memory geo index, one box per geohash cell. Watch frame 2 fail before frame 3 fixes it.`,leftLabel:`pings / requests`,rightLabel:`in-memory geo index`,frames:[{note:`Every driver pings its position every 4 seconds. The location service geohashes each ping and drops the driver into that cell — in memory, no durable write. Note that cells tdr1vf and tdr1y4 touch each other on the map even though their names diverge at character 5.`,stack:[{name:`ping D1 (12.9762, 77.6073)`,to:`tdr1vf`},{name:`ping D2 (12.9789, 77.6081)`,to:`tdr1y4`},{name:`ping D5 (12.9985, 77.5990)`,to:`tdr1vy`}],heap:[{id:`tdr1vf`,value:`D1, D7`,label:`centre cell (rider is here)`},{id:`tdr1y4`,value:`D2, D4`,label:`neighbour cell — 435 m away`},{id:`tdr1vy`,value:`D5`,label:`neighbour cell — 2.7 km away`}]},{note:`The naive lookup: geohash the pickup point, scan that one cell. It returns D1 and D7 and stops. D2 is 435 m away — closer than half the drivers it will consider — but its prefix diverges, so the query cannot see it. This is the geohash boundary bug in production form: not a crash, just quietly worse matches.`,stack:[{name:`ride request @ MG Road -> scan 1 cell`,to:`tdr1vf`,danger:!0}],heap:[{id:`tdr1vf`,value:`D1, D7`,label:`scanned: 2 candidates`},{id:`tdr1y4`,value:`D2, D4`,label:`MISSED — nearer than D5, never looked at`,danger:!0},{id:`tdr1vy`,value:`D5`,label:`not scanned`}]},{note:`The fix: compute the 8 surrounding cells and scan all 9, then filter by true distance. Now the candidate set is D1, D7, D2, D4 — five drivers considered instead of two, and the nearest one is actually in the list. Cost: nine cheap range scans instead of one.`,stack:[{name:`scan centre`,to:`tdr1vf`},{name:`scan neighbour`,to:`tdr1y4`},{name:`scan neighbour`,to:`tdr1vy`}],heap:[{id:`tdr1vf`,value:`D1, D7`,label:`scanned`},{id:`tdr1y4`,value:`D2, D4`,label:`scanned — D2 recovered`},{id:`tdr1vy`,value:`D5`,label:`scanned, then dropped on true distance`}]},{note:`Rank the candidates by road-network ETA, not straight-line distance: D2 is nearer in metres but sits across a river, so D1 at 3 minutes wins. The offer goes to D1 alone, with a soft reservation — an atomic set-if-absent carrying a 15 second TTL. Only now does anything in this system need strict correctness.`,stack:[{name:`rider A offer -> D1 (ETA 3 min)`,to:`claim:D1`}],heap:[{id:`tdr1vf`,value:`D1 (reserved), D7`,label:`centre cell`},{id:`claim:D1`,value:`held by rider A, ttl 15 s`,label:`atomic claim: SET IF NOT EXISTS`,moved:!0},{id:`tdr1y4`,value:`D2, D4`,label:`ranked 2nd (river detour)`}]},{note:`Rider B requests 50 ms later and its top candidate is also D1. Its claim is the same atomic conditional write, and it updates zero rows — so B loses instantly and falls through to D2, its next-best candidate. Nothing was locked for a human decision: if D1 never answers, the TTL expires and D1 returns to the pool on its own.`,stack:[{name:`rider B offer -> D1`,to:`claim:D1`,danger:!0}],heap:[{id:`claim:D1`,value:`still rider A, ttl 12 s`,label:`B claim updated 0 rows — B lost the race`,danger:!0},{id:`tdr1y4`,value:`D2, D4`,label:`B falls through to D2 here`},{id:`tdr1vf`,value:`D1 (assigned to A), D7`,label:`centre cell`}]}]}},{type:`intuition`,title:`The matching flow, step by step`,md:`**Attempt it: a request arrives at a pickup point. What happens between that and a driver accepting?**

1. **Ingest**: driver apps push a location every 4 seconds to a location service, which writes it into an **in-memory geo index** keyed by cell (Redis GEO, or a dedicated sharded service).
2. **Query**: the ride request computes the pickup cell **plus its 8 neighbours** and pulls candidates — typically tens to a few hundred drivers.
3. **Filter**: drop drivers who are offline, on a trip, the wrong vehicle class, or beyond a true-distance radius.
4. **Rank by ETA, not distance.** A driver 200 m away across a river with no bridge is 15 minutes out; one 800 m away on the same road is 3 minutes. Straight-line distance is a candidate *filter*; road-network ETA is the *ranking key*.
5. **Offer serially with a timeout**: send to the best candidate, wait 10–15 seconds, and on decline or timeout move to the next. Broadcasting to everyone at once matches faster but produces drivers who "won" a ride already taken — a worse experience than a few extra seconds.
6. **Claim atomically**: the accept is a compare-and-set on driver state, never a read-then-write. Exactly one of two concurrent claims can win, and the loser silently moves to the next candidate.`},{type:`note`,md:`**Why location writes go to memory and not to a durable database.** A location ping is worthless in four seconds — the next one replaces it. Paying for a disk write, a WAL flush, replication, and B-tree maintenance for data with a four-second lifespan is the definition of waste. So pings land in memory, sharded by region so one city lives on a handful of machines. Durability is genuinely unnecessary: if an index node dies, drivers re-ping within seconds and the index **rebuilds itself** — the data is self-healing by nature. What *is* persisted, asynchronously and in batches, is trip state (requested → matched → started → ended) and the location **trail** of an active trip, for receipts, pricing disputes and analytics. The transferable rule: **match the storage guarantee to the data's lifetime and value.** High-churn ephemeral telemetry does not deserve ACID, and giving it ACID is how you build a system that costs ten times what it should.`},{type:`note`,md:`**ETA and routing are a separate service, and an honestly hard one.** It runs shortest-path over a road graph whose edge weights are live travel times — fed, conveniently, by the same driver pings you are already collecting. Dijkstra is the textbook answer (see the DSA module *Graphs II: Dijkstra, Bellman-Ford, DSU & MST*), but a full Dijkstra per request across a metro is far too slow at thousands of queries per second, so production systems precompute: contraction hierarchies, regional shortcuts, and cached cell-to-cell travel times refreshed every few minutes. The interview-safe line: *"I would treat routing as an existing service with a p99 of a few tens of milliseconds, cache ETAs per cell-pair for a short window, and design around its latency rather than design it inside this session."* Naming it as a dependency with a latency budget is a senior answer; silently pretending distance equals time is not.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Surge pricing: a ratio over a window`,md:`- For each cell, over a sliding window (say 5 minutes): **open requests ÷ available drivers**. Past a threshold, apply a multiplier.
- It is computed **per cell** because demand is local. One multiplier for a whole city would overcharge the quiet half and under-supply the busy one — which is the opposite of what surge exists to do.
- It is a **stream job**, not a query: request events and driver-state events flow into a windowed aggregation, and the output is a small map of cell → multiplier that the pricing service reads from cache. Never compute it by scanning a database on the request path.
- H3 hexagons help here: because every neighbouring cell centre is equidistant, smoothing the multiplier across adjacent cells — so the price does not cliff-edge when you cross a street — is simple arithmetic.
- Tradeoffs to volunteer: the multiplier must be **quoted and frozen at request time** (a price that moves mid-request is a support ticket), and it must be **damped** — a raw ratio recomputed every few seconds flaps, and flapping prices erode trust faster than high ones.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Deep dive 1 — the location-ingestion pipeline, 250,000 pings a second`,md:`**Attempt it: follow one ping from the driver's phone to the moment it can be matched. Name every step, then say which parts you are willing to lose in a crash.**

- **Why it does not go to the primary database.** A ping is ~50 bytes with a four-second lifespan, and the next one overwrites it. Sending it to the OLTP store buys a WAL flush, replication to two followers — **750,000 durable writes/s** once you count the replicas — and B-tree maintenance on a row that is dead before the fsync completes. The trip *outcome* deserves ACID. The telemetry does not.
- **What it goes into instead**: an in-memory, sharded structure of **cell id → set of driver ids**, alongside a flat \`driver_id → (lat, lon, ts, state)\` record. Sharded by cell prefix so one city is a handful of processes, and partitioned by region so a Bengaluru ping never touches a Berlin machine.
- **The write path per ping, five cheap steps and not one of them is a read-modify-write across shards**: parse and validate → compute the cell id (pure arithmetic on the coordinates, no lookup) → if the cell changed, add the driver to the new cell's set and remove it from the old one → overwrite the driver record → refresh a **TTL of ~30 seconds**, so a phone that goes silent falls out of matching by itself with no reaper job.
- **The arithmetic that says this is comfortable**: 250k pings/s × ~4 memory operations ≈ **1M ops/s**, spread over ~20 shards = **50K ops/s per shard** — routine for a single-threaded in-memory store. The same load as durable rows needs an order of magnitude more machines and still misses the latency target.

**Cell-membership churn is the cost you actually pay**, and it is the part candidates skip.

- Most pings do **not** change cell. At 40 km/h a car covers 11.1 m/s × 4 s ≈ **44 m per ping**, and a geohash-7 cell is ~150 m across, so it takes **150 ÷ 44 ≈ 3.4 pings** to cross one. Call it **one ping in three or four** that is a two-set mutation instead of a single-field overwrite.
- **Order matters at the boundary: add to the new cell before removing from the old.** Momentary duplication is harmless — the candidate list is de-duplicated and filtered by true distance anyway. A momentary *hole* is a driver who is invisible to matching for a few milliseconds, which is a lost ride.
- A driver stopped exactly on a boundary — at a light, in a car park — will flip between two cells on GPS noise alone and thrash both sets. Apply **hysteresis**: only move the driver once it is a few metres past the line, not the instant the computed cell id changes.

**What is persisted, what is allowed to evaporate, and what happens when a shard dies.**

- **The live position is pure cache.** It is not written to disk, not replicated for durability, and nothing is lost when it disappears — the source is rewriting it every four seconds.
- **The trip trail is persisted**, asynchronously. Only pings belonging to an *active* trip go onto a queue and into durable storage: at ~50,000 concurrent trips that is 50,000 ÷ 4 = **12,500 points/s — 5% of the firehose** — batched a hundred at a time into ~125 appends/s. Receipts, fare disputes, safety review and ETA training labels all read it later, which is exactly why it earns a disk. Driver state transitions are durable too, and they are tiny: ~12 rides/s × a few transitions ≈ **50 writes/s**.
- **Lose a geo shard and the drivers in those cells vanish from matching.** Not from their trip, not from the app — just from candidate lists. Their next ping lands on the replacement shard and re-creates the entry, so the hole is **at most one ping interval, about 4 seconds**, and it closes with no restore, no replay and no operator. The firehose that made durability expensive is the same firehose that repairs the loss — **that is the entire justification for the tradeoff.** You cannot meaningfully lose data whose only copy is being rewritten every four seconds by its own source.
- The one thing you must still build: the matcher has to **degrade rather than answer "no drivers available"** — widen the radius, relax the vehicle-class filter — and you must alert on a sudden drop in candidate density per cell, because a silently empty shard looks exactly like a quiet night.`},{type:`intuition`,title:`Deep dive 2 — the matching race, and exactly where it is lost`,md:"Two riders, one driver, requests 50 milliseconds apart. **Attempt it: name the precise line where the naive version breaks.**\n\n- Naive: read driver state (`available`), decide to offer, write `assigned`. Both requests read `available` **before** either writes. Both offer. Both riders are told a car is coming. One of them is lied to.\n- The fix is that the claim must be **one atomic operation**: a conditional update (`UPDATE drivers SET state='assigned', trip=? WHERE id=? AND state='available'`) or a set-if-absent key in Redis. Zero rows affected means you lost — move to the next candidate, no retry loop.\n- Do **not** take a lock at the start of the offer and hold it for the 15 seconds the driver spends deciding. That lock spans a human, so a crashed matcher freezes a real driver. Use a **soft reservation with a TTL**: if no confirmation arrives, it expires and the driver returns to the pool by itself.\n- The offer notification must be **idempotent** — at-least-once delivery will occasionally deliver twice, and the driver app must show one card keyed by offer_id, not two.\n- The point worth ending on: **this one row is the only place in the entire design that needs linearizability.** Locations, ETAs, candidate lists and surge multipliers may all be stale. Being able to point at the single write that must be strict, and leave everything else eventually consistent, is the skill being tested."},{type:`intuition`,title:`Bottlenecks and tradeoffs (Part B)`,md:`- **Hot cells**: an airport at landing time, a stadium at full time. One cell holds thousands of drivers and thousands of requests while the suburbs idle, so one shard melts and the cluster scales like one machine. Fixes: **dynamic precision** (finer cells where density is high — quadtrees do this natively), and treating venues as special zones with FIFO queue-based dispatch instead of proximity matching.
- **Write amplification**: 250,000 pings/s arrive whether the city is busy or asleep. The best fix is fewer writes, not a bigger index: **adaptive ping rates** (4 s while moving, 30 s while parked), and suppressing pings that have not moved beyond a threshold. Halving the ping rate halves the entire hot path.
- **Battery and network**: the driver app runs ten hours on a phone in a car mount on flaky mobile data. That forces batched pings over one persistent connection, compact binary payloads, offline buffering, and ordering by **device timestamp rather than arrival time** — because a ping can and will arrive late and out of order.
- **The cost of memory-first**: losing an index node loses a region's live view for a few seconds. Accepted, because pings rebuild it — but the matcher must **degrade gracefully** (widen the radius, relax filters) rather than answer "no drivers available".
- **What you traded**: exact global consistency of driver state for latency and write throughput, everywhere except the claim. Compare with Part A, where you traded publish latency and counter accuracy for read scale. Same method, opposite sacrifices — because the numbers were different.`},{type:`note`,md:`**Close the loop.** Both designs came out of the same seven steps, and in both the architecture was chosen by one number computed in the first five minutes: 400 PB/day of egress made the CDN and adaptive bitrate non-negotiable for video; 250,000 location writes per second made a memory-first geospatial index non-negotiable for ride-hailing. Neither answer was a component anyone memorised — each was forced by arithmetic. That is also your defence under follow-up pressure: when an interviewer says "now assume 50,000 drivers instead of a million", the right response is not to defend the design, it is to redo the estimate and say which parts stop being necessary.`}],quiz:[{question:`You estimate 1.5 PB/day of new storage and 400 PB/day of egress for a video platform. Which line dominates, and what follows from it?`,options:[{text:`Storage — petabytes a day compound forever`,explanation:`Storage does compound, but it is cheap per GB and paid once. In the arithmetic it lands around one twentieth of the egress bill in year one.`},{text:`Egress bandwidth — so CDN cache-hit ratio becomes the primary cost lever`,explanation:`Correct. You send roughly 279x more bytes per day than you add, at about 23x the annual cost, and egress is re-paid at full price every year while stored bytes are bought once.`},{text:`Transcode compute — six renditions per upload is expensive`,explanation:`It is a real bill, but it is paid once per video. Delivery is paid on every single view, which is why it dwarfs encoding.`},{text:`The metadata database`,explanation:`Metadata is a few hundred million small rows. Next to petabyte-scale delivery it is a rounding error.`}],correct:1},{question:`In HLS/DASH adaptive bitrate streaming, who decides which quality plays next — and why does the answer matter for scale?`,options:[{text:`The server, from the client device profile it was sent`,explanation:`That would require per-session server state and server-side decisions on every segment — exactly what makes a streaming design unscalable.`},{text:`It is negotiated over a persistent socket between player and origin`,explanation:`No socket is involved. ABR runs over ordinary HTTP requests for ordinary files, which is precisely why it works.`},{text:`The client — it times its own downloads and picks the next segment rung, so segments stay immutable cacheable files any CDN edge can serve`,explanation:`Correct. All the intelligence lives in the player plus the manifest; the server holds no session state, so a dumb cache can carry the entire 83 Tbps.`}],correct:2},{question:"Why must you never run `UPDATE videos SET views = views + 1` per play?",options:[{text:`Thousands of writes per second contend on one row lock, and a viral video degrades the whole database`,explanation:`Correct. Every increment queues behind the same row, and the lock queue and transaction backlog spill over onto unrelated queries. Queue the events, aggregate in a window, write once per window.`},{text:`Relational databases cannot express an increment safely`,explanation:`They express it perfectly safely — that is the trap. It is correct and it is unusable at this write rate.`},{text:`The resulting count would be inaccurate`,explanation:`It would be exactly accurate, which is the irony: exactness costs a lock you cannot afford, and nobody needs a real-time exact view count.`}],correct:0},{question:`Why split transcoding into parallel chunk jobs instead of one job per video?`,options:[{text:`Chunked video compresses better`,explanation:`Chunking slightly hurts compression if anything. The benefit is operational, not a codec property.`},{text:`It is the only way to produce multiple resolutions`,explanation:`One worker can produce every rendition. The problem is how long it takes and what happens when it dies.`},{text:`Latency, failure blast radius and elasticity — at the price of keyframe-aligned boundaries and a stitch step that waits for the slowest chunk`,explanation:`Correct. Minutes instead of hours, a dead job costs 10 seconds, and stateless chunks let you use cheap preemptible workers. The costs are GOP-aligned cuts and straggler handling.`},{text:`It removes the need for a queue`,explanation:`It does the opposite: fan-out only works because a durable queue redistributes and redelivers chunk jobs.`}],correct:2},{question:`One million drivers ping every 4 seconds; the platform serves 1 million rides a day. Which number shapes the architecture?`,options:[{text:`Ride throughput — about 12 per second on average`,explanation:`Twelve per second is trivial. If that were the shaping number, a single database server would be the whole design.`},{text:`250,000 location writes per second — roughly 5,000x the ride rate`,explanation:`Correct. The business event is rare and the telemetry is a firehose, so the entire hot path is built around absorbing tiny high-churn writes and querying them spatially.`},{text:`The bandwidth of the pings, about 12.5 MB/s`,explanation:`The bytes are trivial. What hurts is index churn — the spatial index is effectively rewritten every four seconds.`}],correct:1},{question:`Two drivers 10 metres apart have geohashes sharing only 4 characters. What is going on and what do you do?`,options:[{text:`They straddle a cell boundary — so every lookup queries the 8 neighbouring cells too, then filters by true distance`,explanation:`Correct. Prefix similarity is not distance. Scanning the 9-cell block and filtering on real distance is what every production geo query does.`},{text:`The geohash implementation has a bug`,explanation:`It is behaving exactly as specified. The divergence at boundaries is inherent to any grid encoding, not an implementation defect.`},{text:`Increase the precision until the two hashes agree`,explanation:`Backwards: higher precision means smaller cells and therefore MORE boundaries. It moves the problem, it never removes it.`},{text:`Fall back to lat/lon BETWEEN queries for these cases`,explanation:`That is the one-dimensional-index scan geohashing exists to avoid, and it would be the hottest path in the system.`}],correct:0},{question:`Two riders request rides 50 ms apart and both matchers pick the same driver. Which mechanism actually prevents a double assignment?`,options:[{text:`Broadcast the offer to all nearby drivers and let whoever accepts first win`,explanation:`That is a different race, and it regularly produces drivers who accept a ride that is already gone.`},{text:`Read the driver state, confirm it is available, then write assigned`,explanation:`The textbook read-then-write race: both requests read available before either writes, so both proceed.`},{text:`A conditional update (WHERE state = available) or a set-if-absent reservation — one atomic step, where zero rows affected means you lost`,explanation:`Correct. Compare-and-set makes exactly one claim win, and the loser falls through to its next candidate with no retry loop.`},{text:`Lock the driver row for the whole 15-second decision window`,explanation:`A lock spanning a human decision is a liability: a crashed matcher freezes a real driver. Use a TTL reservation that expires by itself.`}],correct:2},{question:`Why do driver location pings go into memory rather than a durable database?`,options:[{text:`Because relational databases cannot store coordinates`,explanation:`They can — PostGIS and spatial indexes exist. Capability is not the issue.`},{text:`Because the data is worthless within 4 seconds and rebuilds itself from the next ping — durability you never use is pure cost`,explanation:`Correct. WAL flushes, replication and index maintenance for data with a four-second lifespan is waste; if a node dies, the next round of pings restores the index.`},{text:`Because memory is cheaper per byte than disk`,explanation:`Memory is far more expensive per byte. It wins here on write latency and churn tolerance, not price.`}],correct:1}],interviewQuestions:[{question:`Case: design the upload-to-playable path for a video platform. Walk it end to end and tell me what is synchronous and what is not.`,answer:`The client calls POST /videos; the API writes a row with status=uploading and returns a pre-signed URL, so the bytes go client to blob storage directly and never traverse my app servers — resumable in chunks so a dropped connection does not restart a gigabyte. Blob storage fires an object-created event which enqueues a transcoding job on a durable queue: upload and processing are now decoupled, and the entire transcoding fleet can be down without breaking uploads. Workers split the source on keyframe boundaries and fan out chunk jobs, encoding the rendition ladder (240p through 4K plus audio and thumbnails), segment each rendition into 2-10 second chunks aligned across renditions, and write a manifest. A stitch step waits for all chunks, then metadata flips to status=ready and the CDN serves from blob storage. Synchronous: only the initial metadata write and the URL handout, a few milliseconds. Everything expensive is asynchronous, and the visible cost is the "processing…" delay of minutes — which I would defend as the correct trade, because the alternative is a multi-minute CPU job sitting on a request path.`,isCaseBased:!0},{question:`Defend with numbers: why is egress the cost model for a video platform rather than storage?`,answer:`Take 500k uploads a day at ~1 GB of source, times about 3 for the rendition ladder plus the retained source: roughly 1.5 PB of new storage per day. Now the read side: 500M daily users, 40 minutes each, ~3 Mbps average, which is about 400 PB per day of egress — around 279 times the bytes added. At about $0.01/GB of CDN egress that is roughly $1.5B a year, against tens of millions for storage: about 23x. The asymmetry is worse than the ratio suggests, because stored bytes are bought once and egress is re-paid at full price every year. Three consequences: cache-hit ratio is the primary lever (96% to 98% halves origin traffic), encoder efficiency matters only through its effect on bitrate, and re-encoding the catalogue to a better codec is worth real money — but only popularity-weighted, since the top 1% of videos carries most of the bytes.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Explain adaptive bitrate streaming. Why is the manifest the thing your API returns?`,answer:`The video is encoded at several bitrate rungs, each cut into 2-10 second segments aligned on identical timestamps so a player can switch rungs cleanly between segments. The manifest — .m3u8 for HLS, .mpd for DASH — is a text file listing every rung and every segment URL; the player fetches it first and it is the only stateful-looking piece of the format, which is why the API hands out a manifest URL rather than a video URL. The client, not the server, chooses quality: it times how long the last segment took, estimates bandwidth, and requests the next segment at the highest sustainable rung. That is why quality drops in a lift and recovers afterwards, and why playback degrades instead of stopping. The scaling consequence is the real answer: segments are immutable cacheable HTTP objects with no per-viewer server state, so a dumb CDN edge can serve tens of Tbps. Cost: storage multiplied by the number of rungs, and a few seconds of latency before the player converges on the right one.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Why fan transcoding out into chunk jobs, and what breaks when you do?`,answer:`Three reasons. Latency: a one-hour 4K source into six renditions is hours on one worker and minutes across 200. Blast radius: a long job that dies at 95% restarts from zero, while a chunk that dies costs ten seconds. Elasticity: stateless chunk jobs on a queue let worker count track the backlog and make spot/preemptible instances usable — they are cheap precisely because they can vanish. What breaks: chunk boundaries must land on keyframes or GOP boundaries, otherwise the stitched output stutters at every seam; a final stitch step must wait for every chunk, so one straggler delays the video, which I would mitigate with speculative re-execution of the slowest outstanding chunk; and at-least-once delivery means chunk jobs must be idempotent, writing to deterministic paths so a re-run overwrites identical bytes.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Case: your CDN cache-hit ratio dropped from 96% to 88% overnight and origin is saturated. Diagnose and fix.`,answer:`First the arithmetic, because it explains the panic: origin traffic went from 4% to 12% of 400 PB/day — a 3x jump, which is why a seemingly small ratio change saturated everything. Then the candidate causes, cheapest to check first. A cache-key change (a new query parameter, a tracking token, a signed URL with a per-user component appended to segment URLs) fragments one object into millions and is by far the most common cause. A TTL change or a deploy that started sending no-cache headers. A shifted traffic mix — a big cold-catalogue promotion, or a region newly routed to an edge that has not warmed. Or a genuine event: a premiere causing a synchronised miss storm. Fixes map to causes: normalise the cache key and strip user-specific parameters from segment URLs; restore TTLs; enable request coalescing at the edge so one miss fetches from origin while everyone else waits on it; pre-warm edges before scheduled releases. Meanwhile, shield origin immediately with a mid-tier cache layer and rate-limit origin fetches so a stampede degrades quality rather than taking origin down.`,isCaseBased:!0},{question:`How do you count views at hundreds of thousands of events per second without melting a row?`,answer:`The play emits an event to a log (Kafka) partitioned by video_id — a sequential append, the cheapest write there is. A stream job keeps in-memory counters over a window, say ten seconds, and emits one views = views + N per video per window, so thousands of writes per second become one. Three things go wrong inside that. Hot-key skew: a viral video puts one partition under the whole load, fixed by salting the key into 16 sub-counters and summing on read. Duplicates and bots: at-least-once delivery inflates counts, so deduplicate on (video, viewer, session) within the window and only count a view past a watch-time threshold; unique viewers use a HyperLogLog sketch, kilobytes instead of a billion-element set. And accuracy: the UI counter is fast and approximate, while creator payouts come from a separate exact batch job replaying the same log. The event log is the source of truth and both counters are just views over it. The price is an eventually consistent number that can briefly go backwards after a failover — acceptable because a view count is a metric, not a bank balance.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Case: design ride matching for a ride-hailing app. Start with the number that matters and tell me what it forces.`,answer:`One million online drivers pinging every four seconds is 250,000 writes per second, against maybe 12-50 rides per second — a ratio near 5,000 to 1. So this is not a ride-throughput problem, it is a telemetry-absorption and spatial-query problem, and that forces three decisions. First, pings go into an in-memory geo index sharded by region, not a durable database: the data is worthless in four seconds and rebuilds itself from the next ping, so paying for WAL flushes and index maintenance is waste. Second, the spatial query cannot be lat/lon BETWEEN — a B-tree is one-dimensional and that predicate scans a planet-wide latitude strip — so I fold two dimensions into one ordered key with geohash (or H3), making "near me" a prefix range scan, and I always query the 9-cell block because prefix similarity is not distance. Third, matching: filter candidates, rank by road-network ETA rather than straight-line distance, offer serially with a 10-15 second timeout, and claim the driver with a single atomic compare-and-set. Durable writes are reserved for trip state and the trip trail, batched and asynchronous.`,isCaseBased:!0},{question:`Explain geohashing, and be honest about its failure mode.`,answer:`Geohash interleaves bits of longitude and latitude — one bit of each, alternating, each bit halving a range — and groups every five bits into a base32 character. Nearby points therefore share a prefix, and more shared characters means a smaller shared box: about 1.2 km at six characters, about 150 m at seven. That turns a two-dimensional proximity search into a prefix range scan on a single ordered key, which any ordered store or Redis sorted set handles well, and the precision is a tuning knob you set so a cell holds a useful number of drivers. The failure mode: prefix length is not distance. Two points ten metres apart on opposite sides of a cell boundary can share almost nothing — I have seen a driver 435 m away share four characters while one 2.7 km away shares five. So a lookup must always take the centre cell plus its eight neighbours and then filter by true distance. Alternatives exist for exactly this reason: quadtrees adapt cell size to density, S2 uses a Hilbert curve for near-equal areas and exact neighbour maths, and H3 uses hexagons so all neighbours are equidistant — which Uber built because it hit these edges in production.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Quadtree, geohash, or H3 — how do you actually choose, and what does each cost?`,answer:`Geohash first, because it needs no special infrastructure: it is a string, so any ordered index, any key-value store, any Redis sorted set gives you proximity for free. Cost: fixed grid, so cells are dense downtown and empty over the sea, and boundary discontinuities force 9-cell queries. Quadtree when density varies wildly and you want the index to adapt — leaves split until they hold at most N points, so a stadium gets fine cells automatically. Cost: it is a mutable tree you must maintain while points move at 250k updates/s, and rebalancing under that churn is genuinely hard, which is why quadtrees show up more for relatively static points of interest than for moving vehicles. H3 or S2 when the geometry itself matters — near-equal cell areas, exact neighbour arithmetic, clean smoothing across adjacent zones for things like surge multipliers; hexagons additionally give equidistant neighbours, unlike a square grid where diagonals are 1.4x further. Cost: a library dependency and cell ids nobody can read at a glance. Default answer in an interview: geohash for the first design, then name H3 as what I would move to when boundary artefacts and zone smoothing start mattering.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Case: riders at an airport terminal wait four minutes for a match while the rest of the city is fine. Diagnose and fix.`,answer:`This is a hot-cell problem. Density in one geohash cell is orders of magnitude above the rest of the city, so three things break at once: the shard holding that cell takes a disproportionate share of both writes and queries, the candidate set from a 9-cell scan is thousands of drivers instead of tens so ranking gets slow, and serial offers with 15-second timeouts through a queue of drivers who are all waiting in a car park produce minutes of latency. Fixes, in order. Use finer precision inside dense cells — dynamic splitting, which is exactly what a quadtree gives natively — so the scan returns a workable candidate set. Shard the index by cell so hot cells can be split across nodes rather than sitting behind one key. Then question the matching model itself: at an airport the right answer is usually not proximity matching at all but a FIFO virtual queue for the terminal, which is fairer, matches instantly, and removes ranking from the hot path entirely. Alongside that, cap candidate-set size, cache the ranked list for a few seconds since the population barely changes, and shorten the offer timeout where driver density is high. The tradeoff I would state: the venue queue is a special case in the code, and special cases are a maintenance cost — justified here because the alternative is a permanently degraded experience at the highest-demand locations in the city.`,isCaseBased:!0},{question:`Where in your ride-hailing design does strong consistency actually matter, and how do you implement it there?`,answer:`Exactly one place: the driver claim. Everything else tolerates staleness — a marker two seconds behind is invisible to the user, an ETA is an estimate anyway, a candidate list rebuilt from four-second-old pings is fine, and a surge multiplier is a windowed aggregate by construction. But two riders assigned the same driver is a real-world contradiction that no amount of eventual convergence repairs. Implementation: a single atomic conditional write — UPDATE drivers SET state=assigned, trip=? WHERE id=? AND state=available, or a Redis set-if-absent key — where zero rows affected means you lost and you fall through to the next candidate. Crucially it is a soft reservation with a TTL of about 15 seconds, not a lock held across the driver deciding: a lock spanning a human means a crashed matcher freezes a real driver, whereas a TTL returns them to the pool automatically. The offer notification also has to be idempotent, keyed by offer_id, because at-least-once delivery will occasionally double-deliver. The senior signal here is not knowing how to do a compare-and-set — it is being able to point at the one write in the whole system that needs to be strict and leaving everything else eventually consistent.`,isCaseBased:!1},{question:`Case: the product team wants live streaming added to the video platform. What changes, and what stays?`,answer:`What stays: blob storage, segmenting, manifests, adaptive bitrate, and the CDN — live still ships short immutable segments to a player that picks rungs, so the delivery layer is largely unchanged, though the manifest becomes a rolling window that the player re-fetches every few seconds instead of a static file. What breaks is every assumption I made about the write path being allowed to be slow. Transcoding can no longer be a fan-out of chunk jobs across a queue with retries and a stitch step, because there is no complete file to split and no time to retry — it becomes a streaming pipeline transcoding a live ingest (RTMP or SRT) into the ladder in real time, sized for peak concurrency rather than backlog. Segment length becomes the core tradeoff: 6-second segments give a robust 20-30 second glass-to-glass latency, 2-second or chunked-transfer low-latency HLS gets to 3-5 seconds but multiplies request rates and cuts the player buffer, which makes rebuffering far more likely on weak networks. Failure handling inverts: you cannot retry, so you degrade — drop to a lower rung, or drop frames. And thundering herd stops being an edge case and becomes the normal operating mode, since everyone joins the same live edge simultaneously, so pre-warming and request coalescing move from mitigation to core design. If pushed to pick one sentence: VOD optimises throughput and lets latency slide, live inverts that, and that single inversion is why the pipelines cannot be shared.`,isCaseBased:!0}],flashcards:[{front:`Video platform: the number that shapes the design`,back:`Egress, not storage. ~1.5 PB/day stored vs ~400 PB/day sent — 279x the bytes and ~23x the annual cost, re-paid every year. So CDN cache-hit ratio is the cost model.`},{front:`The video upload pipeline in one breath`,back:`Pre-signed URL: client uploads straight to blob storage → object-created event enqueues a job → workers fan out chunk transcodes into the rendition ladder → segment + write manifest → blob storage + CDN → status = ready.`},{front:`Why transcode as parallel chunk jobs?`,back:`Latency (minutes not hours), small failure blast radius (retry 10 s not 2 h), elasticity on preemptible workers. Costs: cuts must be keyframe-aligned, and the stitch waits for the slowest chunk.`},{front:`Adaptive bitrate (HLS/DASH) in one line`,back:`Same video at several bitrate rungs, cut into aligned 2-10 s segments; the CLIENT times its downloads and picks the next rung from the manifest. Server is stateless files, so any CDN can serve it.`},{front:`View counts at scale`,back:`Never UPDATE views = views + 1 (single hot row lock). Event → queue → windowed aggregation → one batched +N per window. Eventually consistent; salt the key for viral videos; exact numbers come from a separate batch replay.`},{front:`Video bottlenecks worth naming`,back:`Cold long-tail content is a permanent cache miss; a premiere causes a synchronised miss storm (pre-warm + request coalescing); unpopular renditions should tier to cold storage and be regenerated from the retained source.`},{front:`Uber: the number that shapes the design`,back:`1M drivers pinging every 4 s = 250,000 writes/s, about 5,000x the ride rate. The design is telemetry absorption plus spatial query, not ride throughput.`},{front:`Why lat/lon BETWEEN fails`,back:`A B-tree is one-dimensional: an index on lat returns a latitude strip wrapping the planet, then filters row by row. Composite (lat, lon) does not help since lat is a continuous float. Fold 2D into 1 ordered key instead.`},{front:`Geohash + its honest edge case`,back:`Interleave lon/lat bits, 5 bits per base32 char, so nearby points share a prefix and a prefix query is a range scan. But prefix length is NOT distance — points across a cell boundary diverge, so always query the 8 neighbouring cells and filter by true distance.`},{front:`The one write in Uber that must be strict`,back:`The driver claim. An atomic compare-and-set (WHERE state = available) or a TTL set-if-absent reservation — zero rows means you lost. Never a lock held across the driver deciding. Everything else may be stale.`}],mindmapMarkdown:`- Case Studies: YouTube/Netflix & Uber
  - Method: requirements → estimation → API → data model → HLD → 2 deep dives → bottlenecks
  - Part A: video platform
    - Read skew ~1 upload per 10k views; startup under 2 s, no buffering
    - Estimation: 1.5 PB/day stored vs 400 PB/day egress (279x, 83 Tbps peak)
    - Egress ~23x storage cost yearly → CDN hit ratio IS the cost model
    - Upload: pre-signed URL to blob storage → object event → durable queue
    - Chunk fan-out: latency, blast radius, spot workers; keyframe cuts, straggler stitch
    - ABR (HLS/DASH): aligned segments per rung, manifest first, client picks quality
    - CDN: 96% → 98% halves origin; Netflix Open Connect inside ISPs
    - Metadata relational + search index; never synchronous UPDATE views+1 (hot row)
    - Counters: queue → windowed aggregate → batched +N; salt, dedupe, HyperLogLog
    - Deep dive: at-least-once + idempotent paths, backoff, dead letter queue
    - Bottlenecks: cold tail, premiere herd (pre-warm + coalescing), storage tiering
  - Part B: ride-hailing
    - Match latency, geographic locality, availability over consistency
    - Estimation: 250k location writes/s = ~5,000x the ride QPS
    - lat/lon BETWEEN fails: a B-tree is one-dimensional
    - Geohash: interleave lon/lat bits → shared prefix → range scan
    - Prefix length is NOT distance → query the 9-cell block, filter by true distance
    - Quadtree (density), S2 (Hilbert curve), H3 (hexagons, equidistant neighbours)
    - Flow: pings → in-memory index → 9 cells → filter → rank by ETA not distance
    - Serial offers with timeout; atomic claim or TTL reservation wins the race
    - Memory not durable DB: 4 s lifetime, self-healing; trip state persisted async
    - ETA: road graph + live traffic (see DSA Dijkstra), cached per cell-pair
    - Surge: requests/drivers per cell per window, damped, frozen at quote time
    - Bottlenecks: hot cells (airport, stadium), write amplification, battery/network
  - Closing tradeoff
    - Video: publish latency + stale counters bought read scale
    - Uber: everything stale except one linearizable claim`};export{e as default};